GAL22V10
AUDIO65

CLK PHI2 RW RESET A8 A9 A10 A11 A12 A13 A14 GND
A15 EN NC NREQ Q0 Q1 Q2 DONE BDIR CAPTURE RDY VCC

/NREQ = A15 * A14 * /A13 * /A12 * /A11 * /A10 * /A9 * A8 * /RW * RESET
AR = NREQ

Q0.R = /Q2 * /Q1 * /Q0 * PHI2
     + /Q2 * /Q1 * Q0
     + Q2 * Q1
Q1.R = /Q2 * Q0
     + Q1 * /Q0
Q2.R = Q2
     + Q1 * /Q0
BDIR.R = Q1 * /Q0
DONE.R = Q2 * /Q1

/CAPTURE = PHI2 * A15 * A14 * /A13 * /A12 * /A11 * /A10 * /A9 * A8 * /RW * RESET * /Q2 * /Q1
         + PHI2 * A15 * A14 * /A13 * /A12 * /A11 * /A10 * /A9 * A8 * /RW * RESET * /Q2 * Q0

EN = A15 * A14 * /A13 * /A12 * /A11 * /A10 * /A9 * A8 * /RW * RESET * PHI2
   + A15 * A14 * /A13 * /A12 * /A11 * /A10 * /A9 * A8 * /RW * RESET * Q0
   + A15 * A14 * /A13 * /A12 * /A11 * /A10 * /A9 * A8 * /RW * RESET * Q1
   + A15 * A14 * /A13 * /A12 * /A11 * /A10 * /A9 * A8 * /RW * RESET * Q2

RDY.T = DONE
RDY.E = EN

DESCRIPTION
SolderDemon 6502 Audio r1, U3: hardware-timed YM2149F write interface,
the 6502 counterpart of the SolderDemon m68k Audio sequencer. Program as
ATF22V10C-7PX (7.5 ns); disable the power-down option.

A CPU write to C100-C1FF (A0-A7 not decoded here; A0 drives BC2 directly,
BC1 is grounded) is a request. RDY goes low in PHI2 high, so the W65C02S holds
the write cycle and repeats it until the card lets go. U2 (74HCT273)
latches D0-D7 on every rising CAPTURE, i.e. at every PHI2 fall of the
held cycle, until the counter reaches state 3.

Q2:Q0 count CLK (1.8432 MHz, the PSG clock) in Gray code
000 001 011 010 110 111 101 100 and stay at 100. They leave 000 only on
an edge that sees PHI2 high, so the first write cycle has driven its data
before the window closes, at any CPU clock. States 4-5 hold BDIR high for
two clocks (1.09 us); state 7 raises DONE one clock after BDIR has fallen,
and RDY is then driven high (the host pull-up is 3k3, too slow on its own)
until the CPU leaves the address and RDY is released. NREQ resets
everything between requests through the single-product AR term.

RDY is shared, so U3 drives it only through EN (pin 14, otherwise unused):
during PHI2 high of a card write, and from the first counted clock on until
the CPU moves away. The address bus settles while PHI2 is low, and a decode
glitch there never reaches RDY. EN is a pin of its own because the output
enable of a 22V10 is a single product.
